Now I need to understand the stack-buffer-overflow WRITE in `Curl_pp_readresp`. Let me analyze the buffer handling.

The buffer is `data->state.buffer` of size `data->set.buffer_size`. Let me look at the key part:

```c
ptr = buf + pp->nread_resp;
...
while((pp->nread_resp < (size_t)data->set.buffer_size) && (keepon && !result)) {
  if(pp->cache) {
    if((ptr + pp->cache_size) > (buf + data->set.buffer_size + 1)) {
      failf(data, "cached response data too big to handle");
      return CURLE_RECV_ERROR;
    }
    memcpy(ptr, pp->cache, pp->cache_size);
    gotbytes = (ssize_t)pp->cache_size;
    ...
  }
  ...
  pp->nread_resp += gotbytes;
  for(i = 0; i < gotbytes; ptr++, i++) {
    perline++;
    if(*ptr == '\n') {
      ...
      if(pp->endofresp(...)) {
        size_t n = ptr - pp->linestart_resp;
        memmove(buf, pp->linestart_resp, n);
        buf[n] = 0;
        ...
      }
      ...
    }
  }
  ...
}
```

The key issue: `ptr = buf + pp->nread_resp`. The while loop condition checks `pp->nread_resp < buffer_size`. But the `memcpy(ptr, pp->cache, pp->cache_size)` writes `cache_size` bytes at `ptr`. The check `(ptr + pp->cache_size) > (buf + buffer_size + 1)` allows writing up to `buffer_size + 1` bytes. So `ptr + cache_size` can be up to `buf + buffer_size + 1`, meaning it writes up to index `buffer_size` (inclusive), which is `buffer_size + 1` bytes total. That's a 1-byte overflow past the buffer.

